Python Software Foundation and contributors. Python tutorial and standard-library documentation. [Version 3.13 tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/) · [Standard library](https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/) · [Current tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/).

Read the tutorial’s input/output, functions, data structures, modules, errors, classes, and floating-point sections after the beginner book. Consult the exact-version references for pathlib, csv, json, fractions, decimal, math, hashlib, argparse, unittest, random, and subprocess while examining the worked examples. Read the specific callable’s contract rather than guessing from an imported name.

The official tutorial says it assumes general programming knowledge; it is a reference companion rather than the first reading for a complete beginner. Examples here use a documented Python 3.13 runtime for validation. A reader using another runtime records its exact version and checks the relevant contracts instead of assuming identical behavior from a generic command name.

PSF documentation and example terms apply to the source; preserve their notices before reuse. Official tutorial and numerical sections checked on 29 October 2026. References only; no interpreter or documentation collection is bundled.

**Return:** [Reproducible-computation sources — books, research, and primary manuals](../Sources.lingua#r_7RY36NATQ0VQE5JMJAMF6KXK3K).

**Computing-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected official [Python 3.14 type documentation](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/stdtypes.html), [control-flow tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/controlflow.html) and [exception reference](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/exceptions.html) were checked for integer/Boolean distinctions, text/bytes, iteration, return and input failures. Those web pages currently identify documentation patch 3.14.10. The new stock-tracing example was actually verified with Python 3.14.8; record that executed version separately from the evolving documentation page. The original reproducible-computation examples retain their previously recorded runtime. This is a bounded inspection, not an audit of all documentation or the learner's completed exercises.


**Second computing-block inspection — 31 October 2026:** inspected the official Python 3.14 [data-structures tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/datastructures.html), [built-in mapping/set contracts](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/stdtypes.html#mapping-types-dict), [copy reference](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/copy.html), [sorting guide](https://docs.python.org/3.14/howto/sorting.html) and [bisect reference](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/bisect.html). The bounded passages support concrete container behavior, hashable keys, dictionary insertion order, shallow copying, stable sorting and insertion boundaries. The pages identify documentation patch 3.14.10; both new catalog programs were executed with Python 3.14.8. The original examples retain their recorded versions. These are selected reference inspections, not a full documentation or performance audit.


**Third computing-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** inspected the official [Python 3.14 built-in types](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/stdtypes.html), including integer/Boolean, bitwise and sequence distinctions, and its [floating-point tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/floatingpoint.html). The unversioned tutorial opened as documentation 3.15.2; the common examples instead use the explicitly versioned 3.14 reference, whose page identifies 3.14.10. Record the separately executed runtime for the original programs; documentation version is not execution evidence. The original ordered-loop summation and prior examples remain unchanged. Fixed-width arithmetic in the new machine is explicitly implemented atop unbounded Python integers. This is bounded reference inspection, not a whole-language, compiler or hardware-conformance audit; original PSF/component terms and external-reference policy remain.


**Fifth computing-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** checked the explicitly versioned [Python 3.14 os reference](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/os.html), identifying documentation patch 3.14.10, at os.replace and os.fsync. The inspected contracts distinguish atomic renaming, possible cross-filesystem failure, buffered-file flushing and underlying platform synchronization calls. Initial unversioned pages identified 3.15.2; they are not the execution version. The original durable-state programs run in Python 3.14.8 and use in-memory integer tuples, not filesystem persistence calls. Their atomic-word/barrier premises are not inferred from these API entries. Earlier examples, source identity, broader assignments and PSF/component rights remain unchanged; documentation is referenced externally.


**Eighth computing-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** inspected selected actual passages in the explicitly versioned [classes tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/classes.html), sections 9.1–9.2 and its binding example; [control-flow tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/controlflow.html), functions and default-argument lifetime; and [dataclasses reference](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/dataclasses.html), generated initialization, annotations, equality/rendering options, post-init processing and frozen-instance limits. The pages identify documentation 3.14.10; the original abstraction study/checker executed on Python 3.14.8. The [licence page](https://docs.python.org/3.14/license.html) remains the source for PSF/component terms. New binding traces and slot implementations are original; no documentation code is copied. A frozen wrapper is not a hostile-code isolation boundary, and annotations do not enforce the represented value invariant. Earlier examples, runtime records and broader assignments remain unchanged.

